### 3.6 Efficiency Correction

In real case, as we don't have 100% detector efficiency (include acceptance), we have to estimate the effect of the detector efficiency on the observable. Generally, the efficiency \(\varepsilon\) for particle detection can be treated as a binomial sampling \(B(\varepsilon,N)\) with a efficiency parameter \(\varepsilon\),

\[B(k;N,\varepsilon)=\frac{N!}{\kappa!(N-k)!}\varepsilon^{k}(1-\varepsilon)^{N-k} \tag{3.9}\]

where \(k\) is the detected number of particles.

